`existsOwnerInHousehold` (lines 258–262) — `!findHouseholdMembers(lastName, address).isEmpty()`. If true and `sharesHousehold != true` → 409; else `assignHousehold`.

`assignHousehold` (lines 303–320) — reuses an existing member's `householdId` or generates a new one via `householdIdGenerator.generate`, sets it on the new owner, and back-fills+saves any existing members lacking a household id:
```java
public String assignHousehold(Owner owner) {
    List<Owner> members = findHouseholdMembers(owner.getLastName(), owner.getAddress());
    String householdId = members.stream().map(Owner::getHouseholdId).filter(Objects::nonNull)
        .findFirst().orElseGet(() -> householdIdGenerator.generate(owner.getLastName(), owner.getAddress()));
    owner.setHouseholdId(householdId);
    for (Owner member : members) {
        if (member.getHouseholdId() == null) {
            member.setHouseholdId(householdId);
            ownerRepository.save(member);   // side effect: mutates OTHER owners
        }
    }
    return householdId;
}
```
`findHouseholdMembers` (lines 326–331) — matches by last name (ignore case) AND `householdIdGenerator.canonical(address)` equality.

`countNamesakes` (lines 295–301) — counts owners with same last name (ignore case) and same first name (ignore case); result stored as `namesakeCount`.

`saveOwner` (lines 243–250) — assigns `customerCode` on first insert, then saves:
```java
public void saveOwner(Owner owner) {
    if (owner.isNew() && owner.getCustomerCode() == null) {
        long citySequence = ownerRepository.countByCityIgnoreCase(owner.getCity()) + 1;
        owner.setCustomerCode(customerCodeGenerator.generate(owner.getCity(), owner.getLastName(), citySequence));
    }
    ownerRepository.save(owner);
}
```
No password hashing and no event publishing anywhere (owners have no password/credentials; grep found no `ApplicationEventPublisher`). Transaction boundaries: individual `@Transactional` methods; the controller `addOwner` is NOT `@Transactional`, so each service call is its own transaction.

## 6. Custom validator (ConstraintValidator)

`@Telephone` annotation — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/Telephone.java`, `@Constraint(validatedBy = TelephoneValidator.class)`, default message "Telephone must be a valid E.164 number (+ and 8 to 15 digits)".

`TelephoneValidator` — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/TelephoneValidator.java`:
```java
public boolean isValid(String telephone, ConstraintValidatorContext context) {
    return TelephoneNormalizer.toE164(telephone) != null;
}
```
Note: returns false for null (so effectively also enforces non-null), delegating entirely to the normalizer's rules.

There is also `PetAgeValidator`/`@PetAgeValidation` in the same package, but those apply to pets, not owners.

## 7. Normalizer / generator helpers (the actual transformation logic)

- `TelephoneNormalizer.toE164` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/TelephoneNormalizer.java`): strips spaces/dashes/parens; keeps explicit `+CC`; otherwise assumes Australia `+61` and drops one leading `0`; requires 8–15 digits after `+`, else null.
- `AddressNormalizer.normalize` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/AddressNormalizer.java`): trim, collapse whitespace, upper-case, expand `ST→STREET`, `RD→ROAD`, `AVE→AVENUE`.
- `LocalityResolver.resolve` (`.../validation/LocalityResolver.java`): city→region map (Sydney→NSW, Melbourne→VIC, Brisbane→QLD), else `UNKNOWN`. Response-only.
- `CustomerCodeGenerator.generate` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/CustomerCodeGenerator.java`): `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`, e.g. `LON-SMI-0007`.
- `HouseholdIdGenerator` (`.../service/HouseholdIdGenerator.java`): `generate` → `"HH-" + first 12 upper hex of SHA-256(canonical(lastName)+"\n"+canonical(address))`; `canonical` → trim/collapse-whitespace/lower-case.
- `BusinessDay.rollForward` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/BusinessDay.java`): Saturday→+2 days, Sunday→+1 day, weekday unchanged.

## 8. Repository methods used

Interface: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java`. Methods hit during create: `existsByTelephone`, `countByCityIgnoreCase`, `countByRegistrationDate`, `findByLastNameIgnoreCase` (used by `countNamesakes` and `findHouseholdMembers`), `save`. The Spring Data JPA implementation is `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java` (plus JPA/JDBC variants exist under `repository/jpa` and `repository/jdbc`).

## 9. Exception handling (@ControllerAdvice)

File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`, `@ControllerAdvice`:
- `MethodArgumentNotValidException` → 400, builds a `ProblemDetail` with an `errors` list (distinct invalid field names) and a `schemaValidationErrors` list of per-field messages "Field '%s' %s (rejected value: %s)" (lines 133–169). This is what fires when `@Valid` on `OwnerFieldsDto` fails.
- `AccessDeniedException` → re-thrown so Spring Security yields 403 (lines 84–87).
- `DataIntegrityViolationException` → 404 (lines 113–124).
- `Exception` (catch-all) → 500 (lines 96–103).

Note: the `@Telephone` failure and the DTO field failures all surface through the `MethodArgumentNotValidException` handler. The controller's own 429/409 responses are returned directly as `ResponseEntity`, not via the advice.

